![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

The `normalizeRows` function does not validate the structure of the elements inside `row.raw_candidates`. If a candidate is malformed (e.g., not an object) or is missing both `candidate_id` and `assertion_id`, it will propagate through the classification pipeline with a `null` `assertion_id`. This can lead to duplicate or invalid task IDs (such as `semkg-review:1325:null`) in the generated review queues.

Per the general rules, validation functions should explicitly handle missing, null, or malformed inputs by recording a critical diagnostic and failing closed. Please add defensive validation for the individual candidates within `row.raw_candidates`.

```javascript
function normalizeRows(fullRawLayer, diagnostics) {
  if (!isObject(fullRawLayer)) {
    diagnostics.push({
      code: 'full_raw_candidate_layer_malformed',
      role: 'full_raw_candidate_layer',
      severity: 'critical',
      reason: 'full raw candidate layer is not an object',
    });
    return [];
  }
  if (!Array.isArray(fullRawLayer.rows)) {
    diagnostics.push({
      code: 'full_raw_candidate_rows_missing',
      role: 'full_raw_candidate_layer',
      severity: 'critical',
      reason: 'fullRawLayer.rows is not an array',
    });
    return [];
  }

  const rows = [];
  const seen = new Set();
  for (let index = 0; index < fullRawLayer.rows.length; index += 1) {
    const row = fullRawLayer.rows[index];
    if (!isObject(row) || typeof row.question_row_id !== 'string' || row.question_row_id.length === 0) {
      diagnostics.push({
        code: 'full_raw_candidate_row_malformed',
        role: 'full_raw_candidate_layer',
        severity: 'critical',
        json_pointer: `/rows/${index}`,
      });
      continue;
    }
    if (seen.has(row.question_row_id)) {
      diagnostics.push({
        code: 'full_raw_candidate_duplicate_question_row_id',
        role: 'full_raw_candidate_layer',
        severity: 'critical',
        question_row_id: row.question_row_id,
      });
      continue;
    }

    const rawCandidates = Array.isArray(row.raw_candidates) ? row.raw_candidates : [];
    let rowMalformed = false;
    for (let cIndex = 0; cIndex < rawCandidates.length; cIndex += 1) {
      const candidate = rawCandidates[cIndex];
      if (!isObject(candidate)) {
        diagnostics.push({
          code: 'raw_candidate_malformed',
          role: 'full_raw_candidate_layer',
          severity: 'critical',
          json_pointer: `/rows/${index}/raw_candidates/${cIndex}`,
        });
        rowMalformed = true;
        continue;
      }
      const cId = candidate.candidate_id ?? candidate.assertion_id;
      if (typeof cId !== 'string' || cId.length === 0) {
        diagnostics.push({
          code: 'raw_candidate_id_missing',
          role: 'full_raw_candidate_layer',
          severity: 'critical',
          json_pointer: `/rows/${index}/raw_candidates/${cIndex}`,
        });
        rowMalformed = true;
      }
    }

    if (rowMalformed) {
      continue;
    }

    seen.add(row.question_row_id);
    rows.push({
      ...row,
      source_row_index: index,
      raw_candidates: rawCandidates,
      terminal_blockers: Array.isArray(row.terminal_blockers) ? row.terminal_blockers : [],
    });
  }
  return rows.sort((left, right) => compareByteOrder(left.question_row_id, right.question_row_id));
}
```

<details>
<summary>References</summary>

1. When validating expected array fields in input data, if a field is present but is not an array, record a validation failure (blocker) and process it as an empty array. This prevents TypeErrors during execution while ensuring the system fails closed.
2. When filtering or mapping arrays of objects, use defensive guards (such as checking if the element is a valid object) to ignore nullish or malformed elements instead of throwing a TypeError.
</details>

